WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.025
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.325 --> 00:00:08.781
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.081 --> 00:00:15.600
Unit eight: Academic Publishing. Today you will describe complex processes using the present passive.

4
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.726
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:23.062
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.917 --> 00:00:24.941
Manuscript.

7
00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:29.354
Manuscript means an author's text submitted for publication.

8
00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:32.623
The manuscript underwent three rounds of revision.

9
00:00:33.217 --> 00:00:35.799
Your turn. Say: Manuscript.

10
00:00:38.633 --> 00:00:39.727
Manuscript.

11
00:00:40.483 --> 00:00:41.508
Peer review.

12
00:00:42.517 --> 00:00:46.722
Peer review means assessment of a manuscript by independent experts.

13
00:00:47.317 --> 00:00:49.177
Peer review can take up to a year.

14
00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:52.331
Your turn. Say: Peer review.

15
00:00:55.167 --> 00:00:56.249
Peer review.

16
00:00:56.983 --> 00:00:57.717
Journal.

17
00:00:58.717 --> 00:01:02.121
Journal means a periodical publishing scholarly research.

18
00:01:02.717 --> 00:01:04.914
She published in a leading medical journal.

19
00:01:05.517 --> 00:01:07.902
Your turn. Say: Journal.

20
00:01:10.767 --> 00:01:11.849
Journal.

21
00:01:12.583 --> 00:01:13.631
Submission.

22
00:01:14.617 --> 00:01:17.905
Submission means the formal sending of work for evaluation.

23
00:01:18.517 --> 00:01:21.422
Online submission systems have sped up the process.

24
00:01:22.017 --> 00:01:24.263
Your turn. Say: Submission.

25
00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:28.194
Submission.

26
00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:29.893
Revision.

27
00:01:30.883 --> 00:01:34.276
Revision means an amended version addressing reviewers' comments.

28
00:01:34.883 --> 00:01:36.883
Major revisions were requested.

29
00:01:37.483 --> 00:01:40.089
Your turn. Say: Revision.

30
00:01:42.933 --> 00:01:43.888
Revision.

31
00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:45.628
Rejection.

32
00:01:46.617 --> 00:01:49.266
Rejection means a decision not to publish.

33
00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:52.976
Leading journals have rejection rates above ninety percent.

34
00:01:53.583 --> 00:01:55.864
Your turn. Say: Rejection.

35
00:01:58.700 --> 00:01:59.794
Rejection.

36
00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:01.911
Impact factor.

37
00:02:02.917 --> 00:02:07.459
Impact factor means a measure of how frequently a journal's articles are cited.

38
00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:11.152
Impact factor is a controversial measure of quality.

39
00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:15.111
Your turn. Say: Impact factor.

40
00:02:17.967 --> 00:02:19.328
Impact factor.

41
00:02:20.083 --> 00:02:21.003
Editor.

42
00:02:22.017 --> 00:02:26.373
Editor means the person who manages review and decides on publication.

43
00:02:26.983 --> 00:02:29.378
The editor overruled one reviewer's objection.

44
00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:32.299
Your turn. Say: Editor.

45
00:02:35.133 --> 00:02:36.018
Editor.

46
00:02:36.833 --> 00:02:40.264
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:41.117 --> 00:02:45.429
Visal, you're interning at a journal this summer. What happens when a paper arrives?

48
00:02:46.517 --> 00:02:54.498
First, it is screened by the editor. Many manuscripts are rejected at this stage for being out of scope.

49
00:02:55.583 --> 00:02:57.641
And if it passes the first screening?

50
00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:05.361
The manuscript is reviewed by anonymous experts. Their comments are then forwarded to the author.

51
00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.227
How often are papers accepted without changes?

52
00:03:10.317 --> 00:03:19.285
Almost never. Most are returned for revision, and some are resubmitted several times before a final decision is made.

53
00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:21.887
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:26.189
Grammar focus: Passive voice for process description.

55
00:03:26.489 --> 00:03:27.653
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:31.325
The manuscript is reviewed by anonymous experts.

57
00:03:32.217 --> 00:03:34.356
Comments are then forwarded to the author.

58
00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:38.016
Many papers are rejected at the screening stage.

59
00:03:38.967 --> 00:03:48.225
Describe a general process with the present simple passive. Sequencing adverbs such as 'then', 'subsequently' and 'finally' fit between 'is / are' and the participle.

60
00:03:48.983 --> 00:03:53.528
Be careful! Don't say: The manuscript is then review by experts.

61
00:03:55.467 --> 00:03:59.196
Say: The manuscript is then reviewed by experts.

62
00:03:59.496 --> 00:04:01.658
The passive needs the past participle.

63
00:04:02.467 --> 00:04:04.875
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:05.667 --> 00:04:07.562
The Long Road to Publication.

65
00:04:07.862 --> 00:04:11.266
Getting research into print is a slow and selective process.

66
00:04:12.267 --> 00:04:17.621
Once a manuscript is submitted, it is first screened by the editor for relevance and quality.

67
00:04:18.633 --> 00:04:25.288
If it passes, the manuscript is reviewed by anonymous experts who recommend acceptance, revision or rejection.

68
00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:31.272
Revised versions are subsequently re-examined, sometimes over several cycles.

69
00:04:32.267 --> 00:04:38.585
Although a journal's impact factor signals prestige, it is increasingly criticised as a crude measure of quality.

70
00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:43.679
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:44.517 --> 00:04:47.526
The manuscript is blank by anonymous experts.

72
00:04:47.826 --> 00:04:51.758
A, reviewing. B, reviewed. C, review.

73
00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:57.265
The answer is B: reviewed.

74
00:04:58.417 --> 00:05:00.497
Comments blank forwarded to the author.

75
00:05:00.797 --> 00:05:04.254
A, are then. B, then are being. C, is then.

76
00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.649
The answer is A: are then.

77
00:05:10.783 --> 00:05:14.571
A measure of how often a journal's articles are cited is its blank.

78
00:05:14.871 --> 00:05:19.302
A, impact factor. B, submission rate. C, revision.

79
00:05:22.867 --> 00:05:25.772
The answer is A: impact factor.

80
00:05:26.917 --> 00:05:28.870
Which collocation is correct?

81
00:05:29.170 --> 00:05:36.178
A, request major revisions. B, make major revisions requests. C, do major revisions.

82
00:05:39.733 --> 00:05:42.650
The answer is A: request major revisions.

83
00:05:43.817 --> 00:05:47.128
According to the reading, who screens a manuscript first?

84
00:05:47.428 --> 00:05:52.115
A, anonymous reviewers. B, the editor. C, the author.

85
00:05:55.667 --> 00:05:57.852
The answer is B: the editor.

86
00:05:59.067 --> 00:06:02.137
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:02.983 --> 00:06:06.481
Why is peer review considered essential to academic publishing?

88
00:06:23.283 --> 00:06:25.086
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:25.386 --> 00:06:56.397
Peer review is considered essential because research is examined by independent experts before it is made public. Flawed methods, weak statistics or exaggerated claims can be identified and corrected. For example, a reviewer may notice that a sample is too small to support the conclusion. That said, the system is slow, and reviewers sometimes disagree. Nevertheless, without it, readers would have far less reason to trust what they read.

90
00:06:56.697 --> 00:06:58.383
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:59.283 --> 00:07:04.211
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and passive voice for process description.

92
00:07:04.511 --> 00:07:09.880
Today's value is intellectual rigor. Hold your ideas to the highest standard of evidence.

93
00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:12.624
See you in Unit nine. Goodbye!

94
00:07:12.924 --> 00:07:15.913
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

